{
  "assessment": "This change introduces a new implementation of a concurrent counter guarded by a `Mutex`, but deliberately splits the read and write operations in `increment` with an explicit thread yield between them. Because the mutex lock is released after reading the current value, multiple threads can interleave their reads before either writes back its incremented result, causing lost updates. The implementation fails to atomically perform the required read-modify-write sequence, leaving the counter unsafe for concurrent use despite using a synchronization primitive.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "Final count is consistently lower than the total number of calls made across all threads (e.g., ~8000–12000 instead of 16000 with 16 threads).",
      "at": "src/lib.rs:21-25",
      "call": "Spawn N threads concurrently calling `increment()` repeatedly in a tight loop, then call `get()`.",
      "defect": "Lost updates due to non-atomic read-modify-write sequence in `increment`.",
      "expected": "Final value from `get()` equals the total number of calls made across all threads."
    }
  ]
}
